//BUG="a float literal whose integer part exceeds i32 is lexed as a field access"
//OUT="2147483648"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `tokenize_fragments` (src/compiler/tokenizer.rs) decides
// whether a `.` is a decimal point with
//
//     if c == '.' && curr_token.parse::<i32>().is_ok() && !is_next_dot
//
// The `parse::<i32>()` is there to tell `1.5` from `a.b`, but it also caps
// how large the integer part of a float literal may be. `2147483647.5` is a
// float; `2147483648.5` is not — the `.` becomes a `Dot` token and the
// literal is parsed as a field access, so the program fails with
// "Unknown field 5".
//
// The value is representable: `x = 2147483647.5` then `x + 1.0` gives it. Only
// writing it down fails. The check should accept any run of digits, not just
// one that fits in i32.

fn main
    x = 2147483648.5
    out "{i64 x}"
